 First, I will analyze the problem description. The original module has a bug in its combinational logic, likely due to race conditions or incorrect signal assignments. The module uses two always blocks with @(*) sensitivity lists, which could cause simulation mismatches or unexpected behavior in synthesis. The bug might involve improper handling of the zero flag or race conditions between the two always blocks.

Next, I will reason through the design. The original code uses two separate always blocks with combinational sensitivity, which can lead to multiple assignments to the same register in simulation. The zero flag logic seems incomplete, as the original description mentions a zero flag but the module doesn't explicitly handle it. I need to ensure the design correctly implements the intended functionality while fixing the bug.

For the fix, I will use a single always block with a case statement to handle all conditions cleanly. This avoids potential race conditions and ensures proper signal assignments. The zero flag should be included in the logic to match the original specification. The corrected code will use a single combinational always block with a case statement to handle all conditions, ensuring clean and bug-free behavior.

